This is a common confusion on your part. I'm not saying "the expected
number of $ITEM you will have found is at least 1, therefore plan on
having one". Of course, with random item allocation that is not a safe
assumption. I'm saying "the odds of not having at least 1 $ITEM are
vanishingly small, therefore plan on having one".

>>Certainly you have _said_ it but you have failed to present any
>>statistically likely explanation, [...]
>Go and inspect my NAO games to see to what degree I do explore the upper
>dungeon, see where I was missing any exploration, and then stop bullying.

There's all sorts of ways one can waste items or miss them - to watch a
dumplog in turn-by-turn detail takes as long as playing a game!

When you were claiming that games might often not have enough *healing
potions to alchemise up large Max HP, you were unwilling to consider
evidence from my games, evidence that would not require you to watch
dumplogs in turn-by-turn detail.

>For completely random effects there's no need for "statistically likely
>explanations" beyond the apparent (not so apparent for you, obviously)
>statistical variances that can be observed.

This invocation of "variances" is in error. If I flip a coin a thousand
times, sure, it is not at all remarkable not to get 500 heads. It would
not be particularly remarkable to get 600 or more heads. But if I tell you
I got a thousand heads and no tails, you'd be a fool not to demand a good
look at the coin.

>>Straw man. It is possible; it is sometimes very unlikely. The first time
